    "narrative": "Our offensive engine today is anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (75.47), who remains the premier play on the board. Facing Montero, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a strong SP_xERA of 4.53 and a +6% bump from his high-leverage spot in the three-hole, making him the only hitter to crack the 70s. **Daulton Varsho** (68.65) and **Will Smith** (62.26) follow closely as high-efficiency starts. **Daulton Varsho** is particularly well-positioned against Kelly, leveraging a +19% SP skill boost, while **Will Smith** continues to benefit from a favorable matchup against Kent, whose 5.48 xERA provides a soft landing for our catching tandem.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical tradeoffs today, most notably at first base where **Jonathan Aranda** (47.79) holds the position despite a difficult matchup against Williams. The decision to bench **Braden Montgomery** (46.15) was a marginal call, but the optimizer favored the platoon advantages inherent in our starting depth over his raw output against Matthews. Meanwhile, **Bryce Harper** (50.87) is active despite a grim encounter with Sale (2.79 xERA); his elite underlying xwOBA of 0.394 maintains his floor even when the pitcher matchup suggests a low-scoring ceiling. \n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, as several of our starters, including **Cal Raleigh**, **Will Smith**, **Alex Freeland**, **Zack Gelof**, and **Lawrence Butler**, are currently \"Lineup Pending.\" Should these projections shift significantly upon the official release, we may need to pivot to preserve our remaining game caps. Specifically, **Zack Gelof** (44.94) is operating right at the threshold of our start limit; while the slow delivery of Gilbert offers a niche stolen base opportunity, he remains the most vulnerable to a benching if we see an unfavorable adjustment to his batting order.",
